sn35.245:9.1 | S: I’ve made up this simile to make a point. | T: “I have made up this simile, bhikkhu, in order to convey a meaning.
sn35.245:9.2 | S: And this is the point. | T: This is the meaning here:
sn35.245:9.3 | S: ‘City’ is a term for this body made up of the four principal states, produced by mother and father, built up from rice and porridge, liable to impermanence, to wearing away and erosion, to breaking up and destruction. | T: ‘The city’: this is a designation for this body consisting of the four great elements, originating from mother and father, built up out of boiled rice and gruel, subject to impermanence, to being worn and rubbed away, to breaking apart and dispersal.
sn35.245:9.4 | S: ‘Six gates’ is a term for the six interior sense fields. | T: ‘The six gates’: this is a designation for the six internal sense bases.
sn35.245:9.5 | S: ‘Gatekeeper’ is a term for mindfulness. | T: ‘The gatekeeper’: this is a designation for mindfulness.
sn35.245:9.6 | S: ‘A swift pair of messengers’ is a term for serenity and discernment. | T: ‘The swift pair of messengers’: this is a designation for serenity and insight.
sn35.245:9.7 | S: ‘The lord of the city’ is a term for consciousness. | T: ‘The lord of the city’: this is designation for consciousness.
