sn35.2:1.6 | S: tongue, | T: The tongue is suffering….
sn35.2:1.7 | S: body, | T: The body is suffering….
sn35.2:1.8 | S: and mind are suffering. | T: The mind is suffering.
sn35.2:1.9 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: What is suffering is nonself.
sn35.2:1.10 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: What is nonself should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn35.2:1.11 | S: Seeing this … | T: “Seeing thus …
sn35.2:1.12 | S: They understand: ‘… there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He understands: ‘… there is no more for this state of being.’”
sn35.2:1.13 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.31
sn35.31:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the practice that’s conducive to uprooting all conceiving. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the way that is suitable for uprooting all conceivings.
sn35.31:1.2 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that….
sn35.31:1.3 | S: And what is the practice that’s conducive to uprooting all conceiving? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the way that is suitable for uprooting all conceivings?
sn35.31:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant does not conceive anything to be the eye, does not conceive it in the eye, does not conceive it as the eye, and does not conceive that ‘the eye is mine.’ | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu does not conceive the eye, does not conceive in the eye, does not conceive from the eye, does not conceive, ‘The eye is mine.’
sn35.31:1.5 | S: They don’t conceive anything to be sights … | T: He does not conceive forms …
sn35.31:1.6 | S: eye consciousness … eye contact. And they don’t conceive anything to be the pleasant, painful, or neutral feeling that arises dependent on eye contact. They don’t conceive it in that, they don’t conceive it as that, and they don’t conceive that ‘that is mine.’ | T: eye-consciousness … eye-contact … and as to whatever feeling arises with eye-contact as condition—whether pleasant or painful or neither-painful-nor-pleasant—he does not conceive that, does not conceive in that, does not conceive from that, does not conceive, ‘That is mine.’
sn35.31:1.7 | S: For whatever you conceive it to be, whatever you conceive it in, whatever you conceive it as, and whatever you conceive to be ‘mine’: that becomes something else. | T: For, bhikkhus, whatever one conceives, whatever one conceives in, whatever one conceives from, whatever one conceives as ‘mine’—that is otherwise.
sn35.31:1.8 | S: The world is attached to being, taking pleasure only in being, yet it becomes something else. | T: The world, becoming otherwise, attached to becoming, seeks delight only in becoming.
sn35.31:1.9 | S: They don’t conceive anything to be the ear … nose … tongue … | T: “He does not conceive the ear …
