## sn35.64
sn35.64:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Migajāla went up to the Buddha … | T: Then the Venerable Migajala approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side,
sn35.64:1.2 | S: and said to him: | T: and said to him:
sn35.64:1.3 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: “Venerable sir, it would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the Dhamma in brief, so that, having heard the Dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent, and resolute.”
sn35.64:2.1 | S: “Migajāla, there are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, Migajala, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.64:2.2 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keep clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu seeks delight in them, welcomes them, and remains holding to them,
sn35.64:2.3 | S: this gives rise to relishing. | T: delight arises.
sn35.64:2.4 | S: Relishing is the origin of suffering, I say. | T: With the arising of delight, I say, Migajala, there is the arising of suffering.
sn35.64:2.5 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are, Migajala, sounds cognizable by the ear … odours cognizable by the nose … tastes cognizable by the tongue … tactile objects cognizable by the body …
sn35.64:2.6 | S: ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.64:2.7 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu seeks delight in them, …
sn35.64:2.8 | S: this gives rise to relishing. | T: delight arises.
sn35.64:2.9 | S: Relishing is the origin of suffering, I say. | T: With the arising of delight, I say, Migajala, there is the arising of suffering.
sn35.64:3.1 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, Migajala, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.64:3.2 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, relishing ceases. | T: If a bhikkhu does not seek delight in them, does not welcome them, and does not remain holding to them, delight ceases.
sn35.64:3.3 | S: When relishing ceases, suffering ceases, I say. | T: With the cessation of delight, I say, Migajala, comes the cessation of suffering.
sn35.64:3.4 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are, Migajala, sounds cognizable by the ear … odours cognizable by the nose … tastes cognizable by the tongue … tactile objects cognizable by the body …
sn35.64:3.5 | S: ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.64:3.6 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, relishing ceases. | T: If a bhikkhu does not seek delight in them … delight ceases.
sn35.64:3.7 | S: When relishing ceases, suffering ceases, I say.” | T: With the cessation of delight, I say, Migajala, comes the cessation of suffering.”
sn35.64:4.1 | S: And then Venerable Migajāla approved and agreed with what the Buddha said. He got up from his seat, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: Then the Venerable Migajala, having delighted and rejoiced in the Blessed One’s words, rose from his seat, and, after paying homage to the Blessed One, keeping him on his right, he departed.
sn35.64:4.2 | S: Then Migajāla, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Then, dwelling alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent, and resolute, the Venerable Migajala, by realizing it for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life entered and dwelt in that unsurpassed goal of the holy life for the sake of which clansmen rightly go forth from the household life into homelessness.
sn35.64:4.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He directly knew: “Destroyed is birth, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more for this state of being.”
sn35.64:4.4 | S: And Migajāla became one of the perfected. | T: And the Venerable Migajala became one of the arahants.
sn35.64:4.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

