## sn35.82
sn35.82:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and said to him: | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One … and said to him:
sn35.82:2.1 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘the world’. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘the world, the world.’
sn35.82:2.2 | S: How is the world defined?” | T: In what way, venerable sir, is it said ‘the world’?”
sn35.82:2.3 | S: “It wears away, mendicant, that’s why it’s called ‘the world’. | T: “It is disintegrating, bhikkhu, therefore it is called the world.
sn35.82:2.4 | S: And what is wearing away? | T: And what is disintegrating?
sn35.82:2.5 | S: The eye is wearing away. | T: The eye, bhikkhu, is disintegrating,
sn35.82:2.6 | S: Sights … eye consciousness … eye contact is wearing away. The painful, pleasant, or neutral feeling that arises dependent on eye contact is also wearing away. | T: forms are disintegrating, eye-consciousness is disintegrating, eye-contact is disintegrating, and whatever feeling arises with eye-contact as condition … that too is disintegrating.
sn35.82:2.7 | S: The ear … nose … tongue … body …. | T: The ear is disintegrating …
sn35.82:2.8 | S: The mind … ideas … mind consciousness … mind contact is wearing away. The painful, pleasant, or neutral feeling that arises dependent on mind contact is also wearing away. | T: The mind is disintegrating … Whatever feeling arises with mind-contact as condition … that too is disintegrating.
sn35.82:2.9 | S: It wears away, mendicant, that’s why it’s called ‘the world’.” | T: It is disintegrating, bhikkhu, therefore it is called the world.”
sn35.82:2.10 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)


